# WHO MAY COMPOSE THE LAYER DIMENSION, AND WHETHER ANYBODY ELSE DOES. One row per law, with the
# templates that compose `layers/every_layer.sqlc` and are not declared as denominators.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A DIMENSION REACHES EVERYTHING BY CONSTRUCTION, SO COMPOSING IT DESTROYS EVERY BOUND
#    DOWNSTREAM. `layers/every_layer.sqlc` is every filed layer, which is what makes it the
#    denominator a `LEFT JOIN` can find a gap against. The moment a relation composes it for any
#    other reason, every consumer of THAT relation inherits the whole dimension, and any law of
#    the form *this rule's reach is inside the reach of what it composes* becomes vacuous.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND ONE EDGE IS ENOUGH TO DO IT. `composition/parts.sqlc` is composed by more relations
#    than anything else here, so an inner join of the dimension there puts the whole dimension
#    into the closure of most of the checker. A membership test reads `pm.layer`; the dimension
#    is for denominators. Same rows, opposite roles, and only one of the two is safe to
#    propagate.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE THREE ROLES, BECAUSE A BLANKET RULE WOULD FORBID SOMETHING CORRECT. A denominator drives
#    the query or is left-joined, and that is what this relation permits. A membership test asks
#    whether one pair exists and belongs on the base table. And `entries/spillovers.sqlc` is a
#    third: it self-joins the dimension with `filing <> filing` to find the same layer NAME in
#    another filing, so its subject genuinely IS the whole dimension. That is why the permitted
#    set is DECLARED here rather than inferred from the join direction, which the compose DAG
#    cannot see.
#
# ⚠️ IT CANNOT SEE HOW A PERMITTED PARENT USES IT, only that it is permitted. So a file on this
#   list that later starts inner-joining the dimension passes, and the law's reach is the
#   BOUNDARY of the list rather than of the usage. ⭐ The join DIRECTION is in the SQL text, so
#   the program that reads the text classifies it and carries it in
#   `public.compose_edge.inner_joins`, which is what keeps this list to the genuine exceptions
#   rather than to every permitted parent.
-- algebra/roster.sqlc against public.compose_edge: who composes the layer dimension.
SELECT a.law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'layers/every_layer.sqlc' AS subject,
           count(*) = 0              AS holds,
           format('%s undeclared parent(s) compose the layer dimension: %s',
                  count(*), coalesce(string_agg(u.parent, ', ' ORDER BY u.parent), '(none)'))
                                     AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT e.parent
        FROM ( :compose(rank/compose_edges.sqlc) ) e
        WHERE e.child = 'layers/every_layer.sqlc'
          AND e.inner_joins > 0
          -- ⭐⭐⭐ ONE ROW, BECAUSE `inner_joins` CARRIES THE SYNTAX. Permitting parents by NAME
          --    would mean adding every ordinary denominator use by hand, and a list like that
          --    rots on changes that are fine. Driving from the dimension or left-joining it
          --    passes without being declared; only an INNER join needs an argument. ⛔ This is
          --    the one that has one: it
          --    self-joins the dimension with `filing <> filing` to find one layer NAME in two
          --    filings, so the whole dimension genuinely is its subject rather than a lookup.
          AND e.parent NOT IN ('entries/spillovers.sqlc')
    ) u
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'dimension_use'
